Medical Knowledge Graph (MedKG)

The LLM back-end of the project defaults to an OpenAI-compatible call to
http://jupyter-02.aml1.id.iosda.org:8888. There are two back-ends available to
use with the source code: `huatuogpt-o1-72b-4bit` and `meditron3-8b-gguf`.

This project uses Python 3.11, so make sure to have it installed on your device.
Create the virtual environment by running:

  python3.11 -m venv .venv

After that, activate it by running:

  source .venv/bin/activate

To install all the dependencies:

  pip install -r requirements.txt

The setup script `setup.sh` does all the above.

This project also depends on a pre-built UMLS index, which is built using
`build_index.py`. It processes `MRCONSO.RFF` and turns it into embeddings. You
can obtain the UMLS file through:

  https://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/licensedcontent/umlsknowledgesources.html

and clicking on the link specified as `MRCONSO.RFF`. Point `build_index.py` to
the `MRCONSO.RFF` file and an index directory should be generated.

To run the pipeline:

  python run.py --mode full \
    --input input_document.md \
    --index-dir umls_index/ \
    --llm {claude|meditron|huatuo} \
    --ner en_ner_bc5cdr_md,en_ner_bionlp13cg_md

To build ONE graph from every document in a directory:

  python run.py --mode corpus \
    --input inputs/ \
    --index-dir umls_index/ \
    --llm {claude|meditron|huatuo} \
    --ner en_ner_bc5cdr_md,en_ner_bionlp13cg_md

Each document is extracted into its own directory under `artifacts/docs/`, and
the results are merged into a single `artifacts/graph.nq`. The run resumes: a
document whose `ir.json` is already there and newer than its input is not
extracted again, so an interrupted corpus run picks up where it stopped. Pass
`--no-resume` to force the whole thing.

To group documents, put a `corpus.json` next to the inputs (or point at one with
`--groups`):

  {"groups": {"endocrine": ["parathyroid_gland", "anatomy_of_endocrine_glands"]}}

Members can be named by input filename or by `source_id` (which comes from the
`<src>source:` line, and is NOT always the filename).

To pull part of that graph back out — one section, one document, or a group:

  python subset.py artifacts/graph.nq --list-scopes
  python subset.py artifacts/graph.nq --doc parathyroid_gland --out thyroid.nq
  python subset.py artifacts/graph.nq --doc parathyroid_gland \
                                      --section "Hormone Synthesis"
  python subset.py artifacts/graph.nq --group endocrine --out endocrine.nq

A subset is a real graph: it carries the labels, provenance and catalog rows for
what it contains, so it can be queried on its own with `query.py`. The same
scopes work directly on a query, without writing a file first:

  python query.py artifacts/graph.nq relations --doc parathyroid_gland
  python query.py artifacts/graph.nq concepts --section "Thyroid Disorders"

Here, `input_document.md` is a document that follows the format specified in

  https://github.com/medical-uc/document-rewriter

That repository also specifies how to run the rewriting pipeline, which converts
a messy medical school material document into an organized one that this
pipeline can process easily.

To query the resulting `.nq` knowledge graph for, e.g., all the relations that
were built by the pipeline, run:

  python query.py artifacts/graph.nq relations

The command

  python query.py graph.nq --list

should give you a list of what you can do with the querying script.

To generate the MCQ questions, run something like:

  python mcq.py artifacts/graph.ir.json --llm huatuo --out artifacts/questions.json

MCQ generation reads one document's IR, so after a corpus run point it at a
single document:

  python mcq.py artifacts/docs/parathyroid_gland/ir.json --llm huatuo \
    --out artifacts/parathyroid_questions.json

It is deliberately per-document. Its distractor safety check asks whether the
graph says anything at all about a candidate, and it answers that from the IR it
was given — so a distractor it clears is clear with respect to THAT document, not
the whole corpus.
